Intuition
A binary tree is balanced if, for every node, the height difference between its left and right subtrees is at most 1.

Instead of calculating the height of each subtree separately (which would lead to repeated work), we perform a postorder DFS. While returning the height of each subtree, we simultaneously check whether the current node satisfies the balance condition.

If we encounter any node whose left and right subtree heights differ by more than 1, we mark the answer as False. Since a tree is balanced only if every node is balanced, once the answer becomes False, it remains False.

Approach
Create a boolean variable ans initialized to True.
Define a recursive function fun(node) that returns the height of the subtree rooted at node.
If the node is None, return 0.
Recursively compute the heights of the left and right subtrees.
If the absolute difference between the two heights is greater than 1, set ans = False.
Return the height of the current subtree as 1 + max(left, right).
After the DFS completes, return ans.
Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n), where n is the number of nodes. Each node is visited exactly once.
Space Complexity: O(h), where h is the height of the tree due to the recursion stack. In the worst case (a skewed tree), this is O(n), while for a balanced tree it is O(log n).
Code
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def isBalanced(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        ans = True
        def fun(node):
            nonlocal ans
            if not node:
                return 0
            left = fun(node.left)
            right = fun(node.right)
            if abs(left-right) > 1:
                ans = False
            return 1+ max(left,right)
        fun(root)
        return ans
